# Response headers.
#
# These live here rather than in netlify.toml on purpose: Netlify only reads
# netlify.toml for a repository-connected deploy. A drag-and-drop or API/zip
# deploy ignores it entirely, which would silently ship the site with no CSP
# at all. `_headers` is honoured in every deploy mode, so it is the single
# source of truth and netlify.toml keeps only the build config.
#
# Syntax is the same path-matching Netlify uses in netlify.toml.

# The font filenames are stable, NOT content-hashed (fonts/inter.woff2), so a
# long immutable cache would pin a client to a replaced file for a year with no
# way to invalidate it. Thirty days keeps the win and bounds the mistake: to
# ship a new font, rename the file.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

# Security headers.
#   * X-Frame-Options + frame-ancestors 'none' - the portal is never iframed.
#   * script-src 'self' - no inline script anywhere in the page, which is also
#     why the theme bootstrap lives in theme-init.js instead of a <script> tag.
#     Note what this does and does not stop. Netlify injects its own HUD into
#     served HTML as `/.netlify/scripts/hud`, which is SAME-ORIGIN, so this
#     permits it and it runs. What refuses the badge is style-src: the HUD builds
#     its badge in a srcdoc iframe, whose inline <style> and <script> are blocked
#     because srcdoc inherits this policy. The badge therefore ends up clipped to
#     nothing, and leaves two console errors on every load - verified on the live
#     site, not inferred. `/.netlify/scripts/*` can be 404'd in _redirects to stop
#     the HUD loading at all; that is narrower than `/.netlify/*`, which must stay
#     open because the function is served from `/.netlify/functions/`.
#   * connect-src is 'self' only. The page fetches exactly one thing over the
#     network, /api/watchlist, and it is same-origin. It used to also allow
#     *.netlify.app for the per-row reachability probes; those went with the
#     badge, so the allowance went too rather than lingering as a hole that
#     nothing needs.
#   * frame-src names the three dashboards that can actually be framed, and
#     names them individually rather than as *.netlify.app. A wildcard let every
#     subdomain on the platform be framed by this page to serve three known
#     hosts — the same reasoning that closed connect-src, applied to the other
#     directive that had a wildcard. Irish Visa Tracker is absent on purpose: it
#     sends X-Frame-Options: DENY, so the page never frames it (a row there
#     explains that instead of opening a box).
#
#     This list and the manifest's `embed` flags have to agree, and the audit
#     enforces it: an entry with embed !== false whose origin is missing here
#     would be blocked by OUR policy while looking like the dashboard's fault.
#     If a dashboard moves to a custom domain, both places change together.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; frame-src https://contenttrackerdashboard.netlify.app https://rbiweeklydashboard.netlify.app https://ucdcsnlmodulepicker.netlify.app; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'
